                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fef7ebb9-b63c-308e-a89c-27196bc52c19",
                                        "entry_id": "3b027bfe-f55d-31b6-bcb6-e0993d9c6a36",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of, pertaining to, or resembling the philosophical views of Immanuel Kant (1724-1804)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3564028d-0f9f-4456-a093-bfba2e1e7b92",
                                                "sense_id": "fef7ebb9-b63c-308e-a89c-27196bc52c19",
                                                "text": "Gurwitsch's notion of the perceptual noema as a completely idealized phenomenon is more Kantian than Husserlian."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f866377e-db27-470b-a05a-89f840e128a5",
                                                "sense_id": "fef7ebb9-b63c-308e-a89c-27196bc52c19",
                                                "text": "Thus Django becomes the carrier of the “public use of one's reason”—the Kantian road to enlightenment given to him by the German “Forty-Eighter” dentist–turned-bounty hunter Dr. “King” Schultz, and represents the fictive, allohistorical beginning of the battle against slavery and racism in the United States."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0fba8ada-2c77-49e3-b9a4-65e10c99b97c",
                                                "sense_id": "fef7ebb9-b63c-308e-a89c-27196bc52c19",
                                                "text": "It aimed to pacify intra-European relations through exchange and economic cooperation. The framework was therefore Kantian: it was based on the principle of peace through exchange."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
